WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.031 --> 00:00:01.774
Today, we're diving into the world of

2
00:00:01.854 --> 00:00:03.718
conditionals and loops in Python.

3
00:00:04.198 --> 00:00:06.062
Think of conditionals like traffic lights.

4
00:00:06.623 --> 00:00:08.466
They help your program decide what to do

5
00:00:08.526 --> 00:00:08.867
next.

6
00:00:09.368 --> 00:00:10.971
If the light is green, you go.

7
00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:12.874
If it’s red, you stop.

8
00:00:13.415 --> 00:00:15.859
Similarly, in programming, conditionals

9
00:00:15.919 --> 00:00:18.063
let your code choose different paths based

10
00:00:18.143 --> 00:00:19.626
on certain conditions.

11
00:00:19.626 --> 00:00:21.732
Now, loops are like a merry-go-round.

12
00:00:22.294 --> 00:00:24.420
They allow you to repeat actions until a

13
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:25.604
certain condition is met.

14
00:00:26.145 --> 00:00:28.091
Imagine riding the merry-go-round until

15
00:00:28.131 --> 00:00:29.054
you decide to get off.

16
00:00:29.736 --> 00:00:31.922
That’s how loops work—they keep going

17
00:00:32.043 --> 00:00:33.206
until you tell them to stop.

18
00:00:33.848 --> 00:00:36.195
Together, conditionals and loops help make

19
00:00:36.235 --> 00:00:38.502
your programs more dynamic and responsive.

20
00:00:39.003 --> 00:00:41.310
Let’s explore how to use them in Python.

